WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.715
This elective is about two problems

2
00:00:05.795 --> 00:00:07.403
that look unrelated

3
00:00:07.403 --> 00:00:09.976
moving goods through a network

4
00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:13.193
and playing a game against an opponent.

5
00:00:13.193 --> 00:00:15.847
Both end in a theorem that says

6
00:00:15.847 --> 00:00:18.420
the maximum equals the minimum.

7
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.050
First, flows.

8
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.301
A bakery sends vans to a market

9
00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:26.650
through three depots, a, b and c.

10
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.141
Each road is one way

11
00:00:29.232 --> 00:00:31.872
and carries at most a certain

12
00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:35.421
number of vans per hour: its capacity.

13
00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:38.789
Six from the bakery to a, four to b

14
00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:39.790
and so on.

15
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.614
A flow gives every road a number of vans

16
00:00:43.614 --> 00:00:46.240
between zero and its capacity.

17
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.935
And at every depot

18
00:00:47.935 --> 00:00:50.900
as many vans must arrive as leave.

19
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.829
Linear algebra writes this in one line.

20
00:00:54.915 --> 00:00:57.061
With the incidence matrix

21
00:00:57.147 --> 00:01:00.668
A of Lecture eleven, A transpose f lists

22
00:01:00.668 --> 00:01:04.360
for every node, arrivals minus departures.

23
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.850
For this flow

24
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.432
A transpose f is minus eight at the bakery

25
00:01:09.432 --> 00:01:11.142
zero at every depot

26
00:01:11.142 --> 00:01:13.178
and eight at the market.

27
00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:15.865
Eight vans per hour get through

28
00:01:15.865 --> 00:01:18.470
the value of the flow is eight.

29
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.320
But the roads out of the bakery

30
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.900
could carry six plus four: ten.

31
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.953
Can a cleverer flow move ten vans per hour

32
00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:31.427
using the full capacity out of the bakery?

33
00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:32.800
Pause and guess.

34
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:36.752
No.

35
00:01:36.752 --> 00:01:39.450
Draw a line around the bakery

36
00:01:39.543 --> 00:01:41.682
and the depots a and b.

37
00:01:41.682 --> 00:01:44.659
Every van must cross that line

38
00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:48.380
and only three roads cross it outwards.

39
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.295
Together they carry three

40
00:01:51.398 --> 00:01:54.066
plus one plus four: eight.

41
00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:57.247
So eight is the best possible

42
00:01:57.247 --> 00:02:00.530
and our flow is a maximum flow.

43
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.580
Such a line is a cut.

44
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.251
Every flow is at most every cut

45
00:02:05.332 --> 00:02:08.651
and the max flow min cut theorem says the

46
00:02:08.732 --> 00:02:11.726
largest flow equals the smallest cut.

47
00:02:11.726 --> 00:02:14.155
The cut names the bottleneck

48
00:02:14.236 --> 00:02:16.178
widen one of these three

49
00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:18.040
roads to deliver more.

50
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.553
Now change two numbers

51
00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:24.168
six vans on the road from the bakery to a

52
00:02:24.168 --> 00:02:25.933
and two from a to b.

53
00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:29.380
Every road is still within its capacity.

54
00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.498
But A transpose g is one at depot

55
00:02:33.612 --> 00:02:38.294
a and one at depot b: vans pile up there.

56
00:02:38.294 --> 00:02:40.578
This is not a flow.

57
00:02:40.578 --> 00:02:44.803
Capacities alone do not make a flow.

58
00:02:44.803 --> 00:02:49.370
Conservation, a linear condition, does.

59
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.440
How do we find a maximum flow?

60
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.850
Start from zero

61
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.589
and look for an augmenting path

62
00:02:56.672 --> 00:02:59.576
a route from bakery to market where

63
00:02:59.659 --> 00:03:02.066
each road has spare capacity

64
00:03:02.149 --> 00:03:04.389
or carries flow that we may

65
00:03:04.472 --> 00:03:06.630
cancel by going backwards.

66
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.254
Push as much as the path allows, and repeat.

67
00:03:11.254 --> 00:03:14.363
Searching shortest paths first

68
00:03:14.363 --> 00:03:17.860
we push four, then three, then one.

69
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.232
Total: eight.

70
00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.275
And when no path is left

71
00:03:21.354 --> 00:03:24.261
the nodes we can still reach from the

72
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.091
bakery are exactly the minimum cut.

73
00:03:27.169 --> 00:03:29.370
The algorithm finds the flow

74
00:03:29.448 --> 00:03:31.570
and the bottleneck at once.

75
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.221
Why allow going backwards?

76
00:03:34.301 --> 00:03:36.770
Isn't it enough to keep pushing

77
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.204
along free paths?

78
00:03:38.204 --> 00:03:41.310
Take a second network with five roads.

79
00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.388
Send everything along the long path

80
00:03:44.466 --> 00:03:47.896
from the bakery through a and b: three vans.

81
00:03:47.896 --> 00:03:50.624
Now the road from the bakery to a

82
00:03:50.624 --> 00:03:53.352
and the road from b to the market

83
00:03:53.352 --> 00:03:54.521
are both full.

84
00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:56.236
No free path is left

85
00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.340
and greedy stops at three.

86
00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.946
But the maximum is five.

87
00:04:00.946 --> 00:04:03.297
Go from the bakery to b

88
00:04:03.297 --> 00:04:06.399
back along the road from a to b

89
00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:08.561
then on to the market.

90
00:04:08.561 --> 00:04:12.416
That cancels two vans and reroutes them.

91
00:04:12.416 --> 00:04:14.672
Without backward steps

92
00:04:14.672 --> 00:04:17.210
greedy can stop too early.

93
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.756
One more link to Lecture eleven.

94
00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:23.908
Add a return road from the market

95
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.761
back to the bakery.

96
00:04:25.761 --> 00:04:29.098
Then a flow balances at every node

97
00:04:29.098 --> 00:04:31.230
A transpose f is zero.

98
00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:34.181
So flows are vectors in the

99
00:04:34.277 --> 00:04:38.226
nullspace of A transpose: the loop space.

100
00:04:38.226 --> 00:04:41.404
With eight roads and five nodes

101
00:04:41.404 --> 00:04:45.642
its dimension is eight minus five plus one

102
00:04:45.642 --> 00:04:46.220
four.

103
00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.949
The capacities cut a bounded

104
00:04:49.034 --> 00:04:51.157
region out of that space

105
00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:54.980
and the maximum flow sits at a corner of it.

106
00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.189
That is a linear program

107
00:04:57.274 --> 00:05:00.417
and max flow equals min cut is a case

108
00:05:00.502 --> 00:05:03.050
of linear programming duality.

109
00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.018
So, a flow balances at the inner nodes

110
00:05:07.018 --> 00:05:09.363
A transpose f equals zero

111
00:05:09.363 --> 00:05:12.230
and stays within the capacities.

112
00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.460
Every flow is at most every cut

113
00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:19.510
and the maximum flow equals the minimum cut.

114
00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.030
Augmenting paths find both

115
00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.180
as long as they may run backwards.

116
00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.889
In part two: games where one side's gain

117
00:05:28.973 --> 00:05:30.652
is the other's loss

118
00:05:30.652 --> 00:05:33.591
why the best plan is a random mix

119
00:05:33.591 --> 00:05:35.690
and the minimax theorem.
